Classification is by name alone: the names of columns, model fields and typed-structure properties in SQL, ORM models, migrations, schemas and type definitions were matched against {{ "the bundled pattern set with this engagement's changes" if sensitive.overridden else "the bundled pattern set" }} (`unvarnish scanners list` shows it). No data values were read: no database was queried; only source code, SQL files and Prisma schemas were opened, never data files (exports, spreadsheets, database files) or configuration files (JSON, YAML, XML, TOML); in SQL files, string literals and the rows of a dump (`INSERT` and `COPY` data) were skipped before anything was parsed; and only names were recorded, never a value. A name can mislead either way (a `notes` column can hold health data; an `ssn` column can be empty), so confirm each class with the people who own the data. This is an inventory to start that conversation, not a legal or regulatory classification.
